lim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00029-026-01204-3\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2190\nL0: Account\nL1: \nL2: cite0†Menu L3: \nL4: cite1†Find a journal cite2†Publish with us†www.springernature.com cite3†Track your research†link.springernature.com L5: \nL6: cite4†Search L7: \nL8: cite5†Saved research L9: \nL10: cite6†Cart†order.springer.com L11: # Towards higher Frobenius functors for symmetric tensor categories\nL12: \nL13:   * cite7†Open access†www.springernature.com L14:   * Published: 23 September 2026\nL15: \nL16:   * Volume 32, article number 98 (2026)\nL17:   * cite8†Cite this article L18: \nL19: You have full access to this cite7†open access†www.springernature.com article\nL20: \nL21: cite9†Download PDF L22: \nL23: cite10†Save article L24: \nL25: cite5†View saved research L26: \nL27: cite11†Selecta Mathematica cite12†Aims and scope Submit manuscript (selecta@math.mit.edu)\nL28: \nL29: Towards higher Frobenius functors for symmetric tensor categories\nL30: cite9†Download PDF L31: \nL32:   * cite13†Kevin Coulembier ^{cite14†1 } &\nL33:   * cite15†Johannes Flake ^{cite16†2 }\nL34: \nL35:   * 194 Accesses\nL36: \nL37:   * cite17†Explore all metrics L38: ## Abstract\nL39: We develop theory and examples of monoidal functors on tensor categories in positive characteristic that generalise the Frobenius functor from [cite18†5 , cite19†17 , cite20†22 ]. The latter has proved to be a powerful tool in the ongoing classification of tensor categories of moderate growth, and we demonstrate the similar potential of the generalisations.\nL40: More explicitly, we describe a new construction of the generalised Verlinde categories \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^n}\\) in terms of representation categories of elementary abelian p-groups. This leads to families of functors relating to \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^n}\\) that we conjecture, and partially show, to exhibit the characteristic properties of the Frobenius functor relating to \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_p\\).\nL41: In particular, we conjecture some of these functors to detect categories that fibre over \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^n}\\).\nL42: ### Similar content being viewed by others\nL43: \nL44: cite21†Image†media.springernature.com L45: \nL46: ### cite22†On symmetric fusion categories in positive characteristic L47: \nL48: Article 09 June 2020\nL49: \nL50: cite21†Image†media.springernature.com L51: \nL52: ### cite23†Homological kernels of monoidal functors L53: \nL54: Article Open access 12 February 2023\nL55: \nL56: cite21†Image†media.springernature.com L57: \nL58: ### cite24†On obstructions to the Euler system method for Rankin-Selberg convolutions L59: \nL60: Article Open access 01 June 2026\nL61: ## 1 Introduction\nL62: The structural study of (symmetric) tensor categories over a field, in the sense of [cite25†14 , cite26†16 ], typically splits into categories of ‘moderate growth’ and those of ‘superexponential growth’, see [cite27†9 , cite28†19 ] and references therein for recent progress in both directions. In the current paper we focus on the former type.\nL63: In characteristic zero, by classical results of Deligne in [cite29†12 , cite30†13 ], all such categories are super-tannakian, meaning they are representation categories of affine group superschemes. By the principle of tannakian reconstruction [cite29†12 ] this is equivalent to saying that all tensor categories of moderate growth admit a tensor functor to (‘fibre over’) the tensor category of supervector spaces \\(\\textrm{sVec}\\).\nL64: For the rest of the introduction, we fix a prime p, and work over an algebraically closed field \\(\\Bbbk \\) of characteristic p. Then the situation is much more complicated, and we refer to [cite31†1 , cite32†2 , cite18†5 , cite33†6 , cite27†9 , cite34†10 , cite35†15 , cite19†17 , cite20†22 ] and references therein for recent progress. We list the results that feature most prominently in the motivation for the current work.\nL65: The symmetric fusion category \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_p\\), see [cite20†22 ], which can be defined as the semisimplification of the representation category \\(\\operatorname {Rep}C_p\\) of the cyclic group of order p, is an example of a tensor category of moderate growth that is not super-tannakian (when \\(p>3\\)). In [cite27†9 ], generalising specific cases in [cite19†17 , cite20†22 ], it was proved that a tensor category of moderate growth fibres over \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_p\\) if and only if it is ‘Frobenius exact’.\nL66: The latter condition has many equivalent definitions, see [cite18†5 , cite36†8 , cite19†17 ]. The definition responsible for the terminology is that a tensor category \\(\\mathcal {C}\\) is Frobenius exact when the Frobenius functor\nL67: $$\\begin{aligned} \\operatorname {Fr}:\\;\\mathcal {C}\\;\\rightarrow \\;\\mathcal {C}\\boxtimes \\operatorname {Ver}_p\\end{aligned}$$\nL68: \nL69: (1.1)\nL70: \nL71: is exact. This symmetric monoidal functor \\(\\operatorname {Fr}\\) is obtained from the p-th power functor \\(X\\mapsto X^{\\otimes p}\\) on \\(\\mathcal {C}\\), where we can interpret \\(X^{\\otimes p}\\) as a representation of \\(S_p\\) or \\(C_p<S_p\\), combined with the defining semisimplification functor \\(\\operatorname {Rep}C_p\\rightarrow \\operatorname {Ver}_p\\).\nL72: On the other hand, in [cite31†1 , cite32†2 , cite33†6 ] a chain of (incompressible) tensor categories\nL73: \nL74: $$\\begin{aligned} \\textrm{Vec}\\;\\subset \\;\\textrm{sVec}\\;\\subset \\operatorname {Ver}_p\\;\\subset \\;\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^2}\\;\\subset \\; \\operatorname {Ver}_{p^3}\\;\\subset \\;\\cdots \\end{aligned}$$\nL75: is constructed where \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^n}\\) is not Frobenius exact for \\(n>1\\). In [cite32†2 , Conjecture 1.4] it is conjectured that any tensor category of moderate growth fibres over \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^\\infty }=\\cup _n\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^n}\\). By [cite34†10 ], this conjecture is equivalent to the conjecture that the only incompressible tensor categories of moderate growth are the tensor subcategories of \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^\\infty }\\).\nL76: ### 1.1 General theory of \\(\\textbf{O}\\)-functors\nL77: \nL78: The aim of this paper, only partially fulfilled thus far, is to construct ‘higher analogues’\nL79: \nL80: $$\\begin{aligned} \\mathcal {C}\\;\\rightarrow \\; \\mathcal {C}\\boxtimes \\operatorname {Ver}_{p^n} \\end{aligned}$$\nL81: \nL82: (1.2)\nL83: of \\(\\operatorname {Fr}\\) based on the functor \\(X\\mapsto X^{\\otimes p^n}\\), for general \\(n\\ge 1\\). We axiomatise the essential properties of the Frobenius functor (cite37†1.1 ) yielding the notion of \\(\\textbf{O}_n\\)-functors. In general terms, an \\(\\textbf{O}_n\\)-functor is a family of symmetric monoidal functors \\(\\Phi _\\mathcal {C}\\) as in Equation (cite38†1.2 ), for each tensor category \\(\\mathcal {C}\\) over \\(\\Bbbk \\), which intertwines tensor functors.\nL84: We say that an \\(\\textbf{O}_n\\)-functor detects fibering over \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^n}\\) if, for any tensor category \\(\\mathcal {C}\\) of moderate growth, \\(\\mathcal {C}\\) fibres over \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^n}\\) if and only if \\(\\Phi _\\mathcal {C}\\) is exact. The main theorem of [cite27†9 ] can then be rephrased as stating that \\(\\operatorname {Fr}\\) detects fibering over \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_p\\).\nL85: Higher versions of the Frobenius functors should be \\(\\textbf{O}_n\\)-functors that detect fibering over \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^n}\\).\nL86: Using a distinguished generating object V of the categories \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^n}\\), a parity involution \\(X\\mapsto \\overline{X}\\) on them, and a canonical semisimplification functor to \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_p\\), which allows us to associate to any \\(\\textbf{O}_n\\)-functor \\(\\Phi \\) an \\(\\textbf{O}_1\\)-functor \\(\\overline{\\Phi }\\), we show in Theorem cite39†4.3.3 :\nL87: ### Theorem A\nL88: \nL89: An \\(\\textbf{O}_n\\)-functor \\(\\Phi =(\\Phi _\\mathcal {C})_\\mathcal {C}\\) detects fibering over \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^n}\\) if\nL90: \nL91:   * \\(\\Phi _{\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^n}}(V)\\simeq \\overline{\\textbf{1}}\\boxtimes \\overline{V}\\) and\nL92: \nL93:   * the \\(\\textbf{O}_1\\)-functor \\(\\overline{\\Phi }\\) detects fibering over \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_p\\).\nL94: We also show that, without any additional assumptions, any \\(\\textbf{O}_n\\)-functor ‘defines’ some incompressible tensor category, in a way such that the \\(\\textbf{O}_1\\)-functor \\(\\operatorname {Fr}\\) defines \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_p\\). This gives further motivation for the construction of \\(\\textbf{O}_n\\)-functors, although we also show that different \\(\\textbf{O}_n\\)-functors can define the same incompressible category.\nL95: ### 1.2 Construction of \\(\\textbf{O}\\)-functors\nL96: To obtain concrete instances of potential higher Frobenius functors, we establish a new connection between the representation theory of \\(SL_2\\) and that of the elementary abelian p-groups \\(C_p^n\\). This is motivated by the fact that the categories \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^n}\\) are defined in terms of \\(SL_2\\). Only the special case \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_p\\) has thus far been defined via \\(C_p\\) as well.\nL97: Concretely, \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_p\\) is both the semisimplification of \\(\\operatorname {Tilt}SL_2\\) and of \\(\\operatorname {Rep}C_p\\), and the latter definition is the one needed for Equation (cite37†1.1 ).\nL98: Choose a faithful two-dimensional \\(C_p^n\\)-representation V (in other words, an embedding of \\(C_p^n\\) into \\(SL_2\\)) and let \\(\\mathcal {D}\\) be the additive monoidal subcategory it generates. We identify an explicit tensor ideal \\(\\mathcal {K}\\) in \\(\\mathcal {D}\\), and show in Sect. cite40†5.3.5 (see also Remark cite41†5.3.9 (1)):\nL99: ### Theorem B\nL100: \nL101: \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^n}\\) is the monoidal abelian envelope of the quotient category \\(\\mathcal {D}/\\mathcal {K}\\).\nL102: This generalises the definition of \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_p\\) used in the construction of \\(\\operatorname {Fr}\\) in Equation (cite37†1.1 ), and relates higher Verlinde categories to modular representations of elementary abelian p-groups, too. Indeed, in case \\(n=1\\), we find \\(\\mathcal {D}=\\operatorname {Rep}C_p\\) and \\(\\mathcal {K}\\) is the tensor ideal of negligible morphisms, so that \\(\\mathcal {D}/\\mathcal {K}=\\operatorname {Ver}_p\\).\nL103: Using an embedding \\(C_p^n<S_{p^n}\\) this then allows us to define, in Sect. cite42†5.4 , functors\nL104: \nL105: $$\\begin{aligned} \\Phi ^V_\\mathcal {C}:\\mathcal {C}\\rightarrow \\mathcal {C}\\boxtimes \\operatorname {Ver}_{p^n} \\end{aligned}$$\nL106: \nL107: generalising the construction of the Frobenius functor in Equation (cite37†1.1 ) for \\(n=1\\).\nL108: Based on the known cases for \\(n=1\\), which corresponds to the main theorem of [cite27†9 ], and our partial results for the case \\(p^n=4\\) (as explained below), we formulate the following conjecture (the first part is Conjecture cite43†5.4.2 (3), the second part for \\(p^n=4\\) is Conjecture cite44†7.3.1 ):\nL109: ### Conjecture C\nL110: \nL111: The functors \\(\\Phi ^V_\\mathcal {C}\\) define an \\(\\textbf{O}_n\\)-functor. For certain natural choices of V, it detects fibering over \\(\\operatorname {Ver}_{p^n}\\).\nL112: ### 1.3 Connection with modular representations of elementary p -groups\nL113: We show that the part of Conjecture cite45†C predicting that the family of functors \\(\\Phi ^V_\\mathcal {C}\\) define an \\(\\textbf{O}_n\\)-functor would follow from an intriguing conjecture about elementary abelian p-groups that can be stated without any reference to tensor categories. Let \\(V\\in \\operatorname {Rep}C_p^n\\) again be any faithful two-dimensional representation.\nL114: We prove that the above monoidal subcategory \\(\\mathcal {D}\\) of \\(\\operatorname {Rep}C_p^n\\) has finitely many indecomposables up to isomorphism (i.e. V is algebraic) and that it has a minimal non-empty thick tensor ideal \\(I\\subset \\mathcal {D}\\).\n"
